A video's native controls stay as wide as the video after a pinch zoom on a page whose horizontal overflow is hidden or
clipped (`overflow-x: hidden` or `clip` on the body or root; #1018, #1086). `LayoutMedia::ComputePanelWidth` shortens the
controls panel when the video's bottom left and bottom right corners disagree about being visible. It compared the
corners' x, which are in layout space and don't change with the page scale, with `VisualViewport::VisibleWidth()`, which
is the window's width divided by the page scale. After a pinch the right corner was past that width, so the panel was
cut to `visibleWidth - videoX` (a 559 px video's controls host measured about 183 px at 1.7 in #1086): the gradient ended
at a hard edge and the buttons laid out again in the narrow panel. It now compares with the layout viewport's visible
width, which is in the same space as the corners and doesn't change with the page scale. At page scale 1 the two widths
are equal, so a video that runs past the right edge of a page that can't scroll sideways still gets a panel shortened to
the visible part (crbug.com/771379).

